      "claim": "A close chemotype with a basic amine and benzoyl-piperidine core showed a very high brain-to-plasma ratio of 16.6 in rats, indicating that this scaffold can achieve robust CNS exposure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "Predicted pass: the query is a close analogue of [18F]F15599 (Morgan 0.8), which shows rapid brain uptake in vivo; the query's pyridine-for-pyrimidine substitution increases lipophilicity and is unlikely to reduce CNS penetration. Multiple additional benzoyl-piperidine analogues with moderate similarity show brain-to-plasma ratios ≥1, supporting meaningful CNS access. Low-similarity poor-penetration records are outweighed by the direct high-similarity evidence.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No quantitative brain/plasma or unbound brain concentration for the exact query molecule.",
    "Radioligand imaging evidence is qualitative and does not directly quantify brain exposure.",
    "Efflux transporter involvement was not directly assessed for the query.",
    "Species differences (rat/cat vs human) may affect absolute CNS exposure."
  ],
